> A question remains about whether or not this should be configurable. It makes the code a lot simpler to just make it always inside the project, but maybe some people wouldn't like this behavior.

I have/know of at least two plugins which store their editor settings per project ([Plugin Refresher](https://github.com/godot-extended-libraries/godot-plugin-refresher/blob/e2b12f448b9fc112fd0f4acdc6de8df6180b464e/addons/godot-plugin-refresher/plugin_refresher_plugin.gd#L81-L86)), and those rely on `get_project_settings_dir` which is in fact exposed through the editor interface in 3.2 to scripting.

I know that we're breaking compatibility but it doesn't have to be a justification for breaking it completely. What I mean is that, returning the constant `res://.godot/editor` by `get_project_settings_dir` would also suffice (***it doesn't have to be configurable***). This would likely create less changes on the diff in this PR. 😉

If performance is a concern, then making the method `inline` would also be a solution.

Retaining `get_project_settings_dir` would also be good for documentation purposes, as it can be documented just like any other `Object` method, hence improves discoverability within the built-in documentation as well.

Using `get_project_settings_dir` over `EditorSettings::EDITOR_FILES_PATH` would also likely improve the readability of the C++ code overall, and that's one of the important aspects when it comes to Godot development.

My suggested change is:

```c++
String EditorSettings::get_project_settings_dir() const {	
	return "res://.godot/editor";	
}
```

or:

```c++
String EditorSettings::get_project_settings_dir() const {	
	return EDITOR_FILES_PATH;	
}
```
